/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186111724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110861 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050494 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110861 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110861 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "a3c6b50b-17b0-55fa-aa1d-2127b664fc63" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110927 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186107453 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110927 #edb/ref 1069 "q-150699" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110927 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110927 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/b8/b884ae8109d723e9277daa7b076d8a4ff52c07c8295e83d360a07bbb7785dd1d.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, $m∠BAD={90}^{∘}.$ What equation could be used to find the value of $x?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110927 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8df2efb5-d399-5afd-8217-2b502698df60" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110927 #edb/ref 1074 "Here, the letter $x$ represents the measure of the unknown angle $∠BAC.$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- The angles $∠BAC$ and $∠CAD$ are adjacent because they share the side $\\overset{\\to }{AC}.$\n\n- So, the measure of the *large* angle $(∠BAD)$ must be the *sum* of the measures of the two *smaller* angles $(∠BAC$ and $∠CAD).$\n\n- This means that the measure of $∠BAC$ must be the *difference* between the large angle $(∠BAD)$ and the smaller known angle $(∠CAD).$\n\nTherefore, the correct equation to determine $x$ is the following:\n\n$$\nx={90}^{∘}-{40}^{∘}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110954 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186107557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110954 #edb/ref 1069 "q-21727" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110954 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110954 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/bd/bd99171c2696038851bf0fdf7297d2e4a60791f6b11083af8cd51baf48930b2d.png)\n\nWhat is the measure of $\\overset{^}{COE}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110954 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "15477a81-cabf-5246-8bf7-8ff2a8a5aa83" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186110954 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/67/67952e3c3d8b030327cbae686da23d466d509de9171242f5ccc4e502f10813d7.png)\n\nFirst, notice that $\\overset{^}{COD}$ and $\\overset{^}{DOE}$ are adjacent because they share the same side $\\overset{\\to }{OD}.$\n\nWe can add the measures of two adjacent angles to find the measure of the combined angle.\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m\\overset{^}{COE} & =m\\overset{^}{COD}+m\\overset{^}{DOE} \\\\  & ={63}^{∘}+{47}^{∘} \\\\  & ={110}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111590 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111590 #edb/ref 1061 34985 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111590 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "168e13db-19fd-5150-9315-8ec1bd908530" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111590 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111899 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111610 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111590 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111610 #edb/ref 1061 40051 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111610 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "f946459e-27fe-5d63-8cb0-e5bef1bfeae2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111610 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110671 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111724 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111724 #edb/ref 1061 8743 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111724 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e5bccd87-45fe-5b00-91e1-b4f266423fdd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111724 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186111836 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111725 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186111610 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111725 #edb/ref 1061 8744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111725 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5e2a85a2-9024-5643-baf3-fdaaf3ccfcf3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111725 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110748 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111729 #edb/ref 1061 8749 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111729 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7fb4b7de-56d8-52a6-8f79-0bd1ed01d553" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111729 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186110750 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111836 #edb/ref 1064 "When two angles share a common side, they are called **adjacent** angles. When two angles are adjacent, we can add their measures to find the measure of the larger angle. \n\nFor example, consider the diagram below.\n\n![](images/f5/f562f3505de0dd2345b29e2baabaf51a7885cbccb5d95e6201b8bc9aec1b1986.png)\n\nThe angles $\\angle PSR$ and $\\angle RST$ are adjacent because they both have the side $\\overrightarrow{SR}$ in common.\n\nSince the angles $\\angle PSR$ and $\\angle RST$ are adjacent, we can find the measure of the combined angle $\\angle PST$ by adding the two angles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠PST & ={{m∠PSR}_{⏟}}_{\\text{orange angle}}+{{m∠RST}_{⏟}}_{\\text{blue angle}} \\\\  & ={95}^{∘}+{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={140}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111836 #edb/ref 1065 2177 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111836 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "2f387075-8fbb-5e3e-9370-8b93f5e8523e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111836 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111899 #edb/ref 1064 "We can extend our rule relating sums of adjacent angles to more than two angles.\n\nFor example, let's consider the angles shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/32/3264d0ab1a40b02915a737dad57e9f26820193d9bdc6b42431ec1ab6508cc523.png)\n\nAccording to our rule, the measure of the reflex angle $\\angle QST$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠QST & ={{m∠QSP}_{⏟}}_{\\text{green angle}}+{{m∠PSR}_{⏟}}_{\\text{orange angle}}+{{m∠RST}_{⏟}}_{\\text{blue angle}} \\\\  & ={100}^{∘}+{95}^{∘}+{45}^{∘} \\\\  & ={240}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can also use this rule to determine the size of a smaller angle when we know a larger one. Let's see an example." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111899 #edb/ref 1065 5701 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111899 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ed758074-8bf2-5fa7-a897-7d4e08bc5413" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186111899 #edb/ref 1067 "Extending the Sums of Angles Rule" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432375 #edb/ref 1080 "{105}^{∘}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432375 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432375 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "03d7c93e-855d-5840-b285-e8b2de86a4d0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432376 #edb/ref 1080 "{104}^{∘}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432376 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432376 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "126909ba-3771-5f25-a383-19d63b8ff86c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432377 #edb/ref 1080 "{58}^{∘}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432377 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432377 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "14bf17c3-9309-5621-b172-5a68815fd9b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432378 #edb/ref 1080 "{114}^{∘}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432378 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432378 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "150dcf2e-cf5d-580c-80cf-4de132de6ddb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432379 #edb/ref 1080 "{65}^{∘}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432379 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432379 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1899928f-124e-5f54-b642-61246e73c67e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432380 #edb/ref 1080 "{35}^{∘}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432380 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432380 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1a2b250c-0086-5b53-9dbb-d1a339f84847" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432381 #edb/ref 1080 "{37}^{∘}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432381 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432381 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1aeb3471-d0aa-5c83-a134-623e7e010eca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432382 #edb/ref 1080 "{100}^{∘}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432382 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186432382 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1ecc7cb4-cbf2-5870-9c08-126e39b3e1c2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [175